                <div class="section_2" style="margin-top: 5px;">
                    <h2 class="main_heading">
                        Important considerations about your type :
                    </h2>
                    <p class="description">
                        In personality typing, it's essential to recognize that individuals often exhibit traits from
                        both personality
                        types scored within a dimension/style, as reflected by the percentages assigned to each type.
                        The percentages are not fixed at 100%, indicating the fluidity and complexity of personality
                        dynamics.

                    </p>
                    <p class="description">
                        Depending on the situation,
                        individuals may draw upon characteristics associated with the type that has a smaller
                        percentage.
                        <b>The closer the percentages are to 50%, the more likely
                            it is that individuals will display traits from both types in varying degrees,
                            depending on context and circumstances.
                        </b>
                    </p>
                    <p class="description">
                        Therefore, while individuals are assigned a certain personality type for the purpose of typing,
                        it's important to understand that personality is multifaceted and dynamic,
                        with individuals demonstrating a range of behaviors and tendencies influenced by various
                        factors.
                    </p>

                </div>
                <!-- section-3 -->
                <div class="section_3">
                    <h2 class="main_heading">
                        Introversion vs Extraversion
                    </h2>
                    <p class="description">
                        <b>It's important to note that introversion and extraversion exist
                            on a spectrum, and most people exhibit characteristics of both to some degree.</b>
                        Additionally, introversion and extraversion influence various aspects of an
                        individual's behavior, including communication style, social preferences, and energy levels.
                    </p>
                    <p class="description">
                        Understanding where one falls on the introversion-extraversion spectrum can provide insights
                        into
                        how they interact with others, recharge their energy, and navigate different social situations.
                    </p>
                    <p class="description" style="margin-top: 5px;">
                        <b>Introverts</b> are individuals who tend to focus their energy inward, finding solitude and
                        introspection replenishing.
                    </p>
                    <p class="description"> Here are some key characteristics often associated with introverts:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li class="list-item"><b> Prefer Solitude: </b>Introverts often enjoy spending time alone or in
                            small groups, where they can engage in solitary activities such as reading, writing, or
                            reflection. </li>
                        <li class="list-item"><b> Value Deep Connections: </b>Introverts typically prioritize meaningful
                            relationships and value quality over quantity when it comes to social interactions. They may
                            have a few close friends with whom they share deep connections.</li>
                        <li class="list-item"><b> Thoughtful and Reflective: </b>Introverts tend to be introspective and
                            thoughtful, often processing their thoughts and feelings internally before sharing them with
                            others.</li>
                        <li class="list-item"><b> Prefer Listening Over Speaking: </b>Introverts may be more comfortable
                            listening attentively in conversations rather than being the center of attention. They often
                            value active listening and thoughtful responses.</li>
                        <li class="list-item"><b> Sensitive to Stimuli: </b>Introverts may be more sensitive to external
                            stimuli, such as noise, crowds, or bright lights. They may feel overwhelmed in highly
                            stimulating environments and require time alone to recharge.</li>
                        <li class="list-item"><b> Creative and Innovative: </b>Many introverts possess rich inner worlds
                            and may excel in creative pursuits such as writing, art, or music. They often draw
                            inspiration from their introspection and observations of the world around them.</li>
                        <li class="list-item"><b> Independent and Self-Sufficient: </b> Introverts tend to be
                            self-sufficient and may enjoy pursuing individual hobbies or projects. They may thrive in
                            roles that allow for autonomy and independence.</li>

                    </ul>
                    <p class="description" style="margin-top: 8px;">
                        <b>Extroverts</b> are individuals who tend to direct their energy outward, finding stimulation
                        and excitement in social interactions and external environments.
                    </p>
                    <p class="description">Here are some key characteristics often associated with extroverts:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li class="list-item"><b> Sociable and Outgoing: </b>Extroverts thrive on social interactions
                            and enjoy being around people. They often feel energized and rejuvenated by engaging in
                            lively conversations and social activities. </li>
                        <li class="list-item"><b> Easily Approachable: </b>Extroverts are typically open and
                            approachable, making it easy for them to initiate and maintain conversations with others.
                            They may enjoy meeting new people and making connections.
                        </li>
                        <li class="list-item"><b> Expressive and Talkative: </b>Extroverts tend to be expressive and
                            talkative, readily sharing their thoughts, feelings, and ideas with those around them. They
                            may enjoy being the center of attention in social settings.</li>
                        <li class="list-item"><b> Enjoy Group Activities: </b>Extroverts often enjoy participating in
                            group activities, events, and gatherings. They may thrive in environments with a lot of
                            social interaction and external stimulation.</li>
                        <li class="list-item"><b> Think Aloud: </b>Extroverts may prefer to process their thoughts and
                            ideas by talking them out loud, bouncing ideas off others, and engaging in collaborative
                            discussions.</li>
                        <li class="list-item"><b> Adventurous and Spontaneous: </b>Many extroverts enjoy spontaneity and
                            seek out new experiences and adventures. They may be more willing to take risks and embrace
                            opportunities for excitement and novelty.</li>
                        <li class="list-item"><b> Energized by External Stimulation: </b> Extroverts draw energy from
                            their external environment and may feel restless or bored when they spend too much time
                            alone. They thrive on the excitement and energy of social interactions and dynamic
                            environments.
                        </li>

                    </ul>


